30
00:02:06,458 --> 00:02:06,951
workflow;

31
00:02:07,291 --> 00:02:12,134
derive the version of a release from a tag
in CI and say what the checkout needs for

32
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:12,424
that;

33
00:02:12,764 --> 00:02:15,649
outline release automation
as the section gives it.

34
00:02:17,489 --> 00:02:23,423
The image. Workflow 6 builds and pushes an
image to the GitHub container registry at

35
00:02:23,463 --> 00:02:26,339
ghcr.io. Three facts matter for delivery.

36
00:02:26,679 --> 00:02:31,717
First, authentication. The job authenticates
with the GITHUB_TOKEN, the short-lived

37
00:02:31,757 --> 00:02:36,672
token every job gets, and needs packages:
write. The documentation recommends the

38
00:02:36,712 --> 00:02:41,628
token over a personal access token, and
says that publishing from a workflow with

39
00:02:41,668 --> 00:02:46,278
that token is also the easiest way to
connect the package to the repository.

40
00:02:46,618 --> 00:02:51,601
Second, how tags and content are produced.
The metadata action derives tags and

41
00:02:51,641 --> 00:02:56,752
labels from the Git ref and commit. The
build action builds and, with push: true,

42
00:02:56,792 --> 00:03:02,094
pushes. By default that action builds from
the Git context, not from the checked-out

43
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:06,800
directory, so files changed by earlier
steps are ignored unless context: .

44
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:07,245
is set.

45
00:03:07,585 --> 00:03:13,001
Third, and this is the sentence of the
video: an image is identified by its digest,

46
00:03:13,041 --> 00:03:17,998
not by a tag. The digest is a fingerprint
computed from the image's content.

47
00:03:18,038 --> 00:03:22,600
A tag such as latest or main is a
movable name, exactly like a branch.

48
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:27,070
So: deploy by digest, and record
the digest and the commit together.

49
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:32,264
The textbook adds that actions/attest can
attach signed build provenance to the

50
00:03:32,304 --> 00:03:34,302
digest. That belongs to Part 7.

51
00:03:34,642 --> 00:03:38,382
One commit per image. An image
tag should identify one commit.

52
00:03:38,422 --> 00:03:42,223
Git can tell you two things that
make that statement checkable.

53
00:03:42,263 --> 00:03:47,100
Whether a working tree is exactly a commit:
git status in its short or porcelain

54
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:51,856
form prints nothing when it is. And what
that commit is called relative to the

55
00:03:51,896 --> 00:03:56,429
release tags: git describe with --tags
and --match for the release pattern.

56
00:03:56,769 --> 00:04:00,651
git describe has an option for
exactly this purpose: --dirty.

57
00:04:00,691 --> 00:04:04,381
It appends -dirty when the
working tree differs from HEAD.

58
00:04:04,421 --> 00:04:09,589
A runner's checkout is clean, which is one
reason the textbook gives for building

59
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:12,226
release images in CI and not on a laptop.

60
00:04:12,566 --> 00:04:18,171
And the connection to video 145: a job that
derives the version from a tag needs the

61
00:04:18,211 --> 00:04:23,749
tags and enough history for git describe
to walk. The default checkout has neither.

62
00:04:24,089 --> 00:04:27,262
Releases, in outline.
Three facts and one trap.

63
00:04:27,602 --> 00:04:31,344
Fact one. A release is a
GitHub object on a Git tag.

64
00:04:31,384 --> 00:04:36,363
In a job, gh release create with the
tag, the files, --verify-tag and

65
00:04:36,403 --> 00:04:42,328
--generate-notes creates it, with the token
in the environment and contents: write

66
00:04:42,368 --> 00:04:47,275
permission. --verify-tag refuses to
invent a tag that doesn't exist.

67
00:04:47,315 --> 00:04:53,166
You saw why that matters in video 140:
without it the command may create a tag on

68
00:04:53,206 --> 00:04:58,040
the default branch, and section
15.22 labels that form 🔴 DANGEROUS.

69
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:01,895
With --verify-tag and --draft
the label is 🟡 CAUTION.

70
00:05:02,235 --> 00:05:08,156
Fact two. With immutable releases, create
a draft, attach every asset, then publish.

71
00:05:08,496 --> 00:05:13,388
Fact three. Tools such as release-please
derive the release from conventional commit

72
00:05:13,428 --> 00:05:18,202
messages. The textbook is explicit: it's
named as a concept and wasn't run for the

73
00:05:18,242 --> 00:05:18,495
book.

74
00:05:18,835 --> 00:05:23,275
Quick quiz. One workflow creates
a release with the job token.

75
00:05:23,315 --> 00:05:28,549
A second workflow has the trigger on:
release. Does the second one start?

76
00:05:28,589 --> 00:05:30,499
A, yes. B, no. Your answer?

77
00:05:35,339 --> 00:05:40,367
The trap. B, it doesn't. Events caused by
the GITHUB_TOKEN don't start new workflow

78
00:05:40,407 --> 00:05:45,313
runs, with narrow exceptions. A workflow
that creates a tag or a release with the

79
00:05:45,353 --> 00:05:49,648
job token won't trigger your separate
on: release or tag-push workflow.

80
00:05:49,988 --> 00:05:55,024
The textbook gives two ways out. Either
chain the work as jobs of one workflow with

81
00:05:55,064 --> 00:05:58,020
needs. Or create the tag
with a GitHub App token.

82
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:03,324
Why does GitHub do this? You heard the
rule in video 143 as a property of the job

83
00:06:03,364 --> 00:06:08,328
token. Think about the alternative: a
workflow that pushes with its own token and

84
00:06:08,368 --> 00:06:10,057
thereby starts itself again.

85
00:06:11,897 --> 00:06:15,513
There are three kinds of names on an
image, and each has a twin in Git.

86
00:06:15,853 --> 00:06:20,819
A tag like main or latest behaves like a
branch: it's a name that moves when someone

87
00:06:20,859 --> 00:06:21,236
pushes.

88
00:06:21,576 --> 00:06:27,244
A tag like 0.2.0 behaves like a release
tag: it's meant to stay, and nothing but

89
00:06:27,284 --> 00:06:29,027
discipline makes it stay.

90
00:06:29,367 --> 00:06:33,941
The digest behaves like a commit ID: it's
computed from the content, so it can't

91
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:35,036
name anything else.

92
00:06:35,376 --> 00:06:39,797
The model you built in Part 1, that a
branch is a ref and a commit ID is the

93
00:06:39,837 --> 00:06:44,669
identity, carries over directly. Deploying
:latest is like deploying "whatever main

94
00:06:44,709 --> 00:06:46,782
is at the moment the machine pulls".

95
00:06:47,122 --> 00:06:50,809
Where the comparison breaks: a
commit ID covers the source.

96
00:06:50,849 --> 00:06:55,862
A digest covers the built image. The same
commit built twice doesn't necessarily

97
00:06:55,902 --> 00:07:00,221
give the same digest, because a build
tool isn't always reproducible.

98
00:07:00,261 --> 00:07:04,642
You heard that in video 149. So
neither identifier replaces the other.

99
00:07:04,682 --> 00:07:09,632
You record both, together, and that pair
is what answers the on-call engineer's

100
00:07:09,672 --> 00:07:10,201
question.

101
00:07:12,041 --> 00:07:15,580
Start at the commit. The build
gets its ID in the checkout.

102
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:20,494
The image carries it in a tag and a label.
The deployment record holds the digest

103
00:07:20,534 --> 00:07:22,132
and the commit ID together.

104
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:26,373
Follow the commit ID along the
chain: it's written on every arrow.

105
00:07:28,213 --> 00:07:28,972
Into the lab.

106
00:07:29,312 --> 00:07:33,357
Step 1: is this tree exactly a commit,
and what is the commit called?

107
00:07:33,697 --> 00:07:39,030
All four only read. No tag points at HEAD,
and the newest release tag is two commits

108
00:07:39,070 --> 00:07:42,355
back. What will git describe
print? Say it out loud.

109
00:07:47,195 --> 00:07:53,022
The full ID of HEAD. Then nothing from git
status --porcelain: the tree is clean,

110
00:07:53,062 --> 00:07:58,743
it's exactly that commit. Nothing from
git tag --points-at: this commit isn't a

111
00:07:58,783 --> 00:07:59,322
release.

112
00:07:59,662 --> 00:08:06,229
And the description: v1.1.0-2-g57c8425. The
last release, two commits since, and the

113
00:08:06,269 --> 00:08:12,599
abbreviated ID. That string is a usable
build identifier: it names one commit and

114
00:08:12,639 --> 00:08:14,802
tells a human where it sits.

115
00:08:15,142 --> 00:08:19,254
Try it now. Thirty seconds, in
any repository of your own.

116
00:08:19,294 --> 00:08:24,195
Run git status --porcelain, which
only reads. Does it print anything?

117
00:08:26,535 --> 00:08:29,957
If it printed nothing, your
working tree is exactly a commit.

118
00:08:29,997 --> 00:08:34,611
If it printed lines, each one is a difference,
and a build from that tree wouldn't

119
00:08:34,651 --> 00:08:36,313
be the commit it claims to be.

120
00:08:36,653 --> 00:08:39,527
Step 2: a build from a tree
that is not the commit.

121
00:08:39,867 --> 00:08:43,394
Someone builds on a laptop
with one uncommitted line.

122
00:08:43,434 --> 00:08:46,356
What does --dirty add?
Make your prediction.

123
00:08:51,196 --> 00:08:55,153
-dirty. Git is saying that the
working tree differs from HEAD.

124
00:08:55,193 --> 00:09:00,440
An image built now and labelled with the
commit ID alone would claim to be 57c8425

125
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:01,471
and wouldn't be.

126
00:09:01,811 --> 00:09:05,529
Here's the same state in the
three-trees picture from Part 1.

127
00:09:05,569 --> 00:09:10,642
HEAD and the index still hold the commit.
Only the working tree has the extra line.

128
00:09:10,982 --> 00:09:13,359
Step 3: recovery, and a release.

129
00:09:17,699 --> 00:09:22,172
git restore on a path is 🔴 DANGEROUS,
so the five answers before it runs.

130
00:09:22,212 --> 00:09:26,005
What it changes: the file in the
working tree, from the index.

131
00:09:26,045 --> 00:09:30,642
What it can destroy: uncommitted edits
to that file that were never staged.

132
00:09:30,682 --> 00:09:35,526
How to preview: git diff for the path.
How to recover: there is no recovery for

133
00:09:35,566 --> 00:09:40,472
content that was never staged. When it's
appropriate: here, for a one-line tweak

134
00:09:40,512 --> 00:09:45,417
made on purpose in a sandbox, after git
status showed that it's the only change.

135
00:09:45,757 --> 00:09:49,424
git tag -a is 🟢 SAFE: it
adds an object and a ref.

136
00:09:49,764 --> 00:09:52,158
After the restore the
description is clean again.

137
00:09:52,498 --> 00:09:57,772
After the annotated tag, the tag points at
HEAD and the description is the tag name

138
00:09:57,812 --> 00:10:02,958
itself: v1.2.0. That's the moment a release
build wants: the commit's name is the

139
00:10:02,998 --> 00:10:04,943
version, with nothing after it.

140
00:10:05,283 --> 00:10:09,981
Part B of Lab 27.1, on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

